{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "Expressed customer dissatisfaction in the review text — i.e., what aspect customers complain about most, how negative the review tone is, and whether that text sentiment aligns with the numeric ratings.",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "sentiment_score",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "A compound sentiment (~-1..1) of the review text. It relates to the 'how negative is the review' facet, but on its own it is exactly the kind of bare/generic sentiment measure the rubric flags — it points at the focus only weakly."
    },
    {
      "name": "dominant_complaint",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Core column: extracts the primary complaint theme (staff, delay, seat, food, value, entertainment). This is the substantive, data-inferable anchor of the focus and directly answers 'which aspect is worth analyzing.'"
    },
    {
      "name": "review_length",
      "supports_focus": false,
      "rationale": "A purely mechanical token/word-count metric. It does not center on any substantive dissatisfaction concept and reads as grab-bag filler rather than a facet of the focus."
    },
    {
      "name": "rating_sentiment_gap",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Compares text sentiment to the numeric rating, a genuinely analytical relational facet — surfaces where written tone diverges from scores, tying text signal back to the structured ratings and reinforcing the dissatisfaction focus."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.5,
  "structure_quality": 0.45,
  "focus_actionability": 0.6,
  "rationale": "Three of the four columns (dominant_complaint, sentiment_score, rating_sentiment_gap) loosely orbit a single coherent concept — dissatisfaction/complaint content expressed in the review text and its alignment with ratings — so the set is not wholly scattered. However, coherence is diluted: sentiment_score is bare sentiment and review_length is a mechanical text-length metric, precisely the generic signals that do not realise a focus. As a structure, there is a promising nucleus (a complaint theme + an intensity measure + a text-vs-rating gap) but it is half-filled with undifferentiated filler rather than a clean, differentiated facet/predictor set, so structure quality is only moderate. The strongest inferable focus — complaint-driver themes — is genuinely actionable (an analyst can prioritise staff/delay/seat issues and probe text-rating divergence), but because the column set as a whole leans partly on vacuous sentiment/length framing, actionability is only moderately supported rather than strong."
}

